# Rank 1418 / CVE-2026-44694 (GHSA-cmrh-wvq6-wm9r, recorded CWE-367 + CWE-918,
# both right): czlonkowski/n8n-mcp is an MCP server that gives a model the n8n
# node catalogue and, through `n8n_trigger_webhook_workflow` and the chat and
# form trigger handlers, the ability to make the server issue an HTTP request to
# an n8n webhook. The URL is built from tool arguments the model chooses, so the
# trust boundary is the model's: anything that reaches the model's context --
# a fetched page, a workflow description, another tool's result -- can steer the
# hostname the server connects to, while the server itself holds the n8n API key
# and usually sits inside the network the operator is trying to protect.
#
# The project knows this and ships a filter. `src/utils/ssrf-protection.ts`
# exports `SSRFProtection.validateWebhookUrl(urlString)`, which parses the URL,
# rejects non-HTTP schemes, rejects the cloud-metadata hostnames by name, then:
#
#   const { address } = await lookup(hostname);
#   resolvedIP = address;
#   ...
#   if (CLOUD_METADATA.has(resolvedIP)) return { valid: false, ... };
#   const isLocalhost = LOCALHOST_PATTERNS.has(hostname) ||
#                       resolvedIP === '::1' || resolvedIP.startsWith('127.');
#   if (PRIVATE_IP_RANGES.some(regex => regex.test(resolvedIP))) return { valid: false, ... };
#   ...
#   return { valid: true };
#
# and its own doc comment says "@security Uses DNS resolution to prevent DNS
# rebinding attacks". It does not. `dns.lookup` returns a `{ address, family }`
# record -- a value, not a connection -- and `validateWebhookUrl` throws the
# address away and returns a bare verdict. Every caller then issues the request
# against the *hostname*: `axios.request({ url: chatUrl, ... })` in
# `chat-handler.ts`, `axios.request({ url: formUrl, ... })` in `form-handler.ts`,
# `axios.create({ baseURL: new URL('/', webhookUrl) })` in
# `n8n-api-client.triggerWebhook`, and `axios.get(settingsUrl)` in
# `n8n-version.fetchN8nVersion`. Each of those resolves the name a second time
# through Node's own lookup, and two resolutions of a name whose DNS the
# attacker controls need not agree. A record with a one-second TTL answers the
# validating lookup with a public address and the connecting lookup with
# 127.0.0.1 or 169.254.169.254. The check and the use are the same name resolved
# twice, which is why the axis is CWE-367 and the consequence is CWE-918.
#
# So this is not a missing SSRF guard and not an incomplete denylist -- the
# denylist is unusually thorough, covering IPv4 ranges, IPv4-mapped and 6to4
# IPv6, and four clouds' metadata addresses. The defect is that the guard's
# answer cannot bind the connection.
#
# bcaba839409d470abeb4a6ad9b361b553a1098eb fixes it on the transport, which is
# where Node documents the control. `validateWebhookUrl`'s result type gains
# `address` and `family` and every `{ valid: true }` return carries them; a new
# `SSRFProtection.createPinnedAgents(address, family)` builds an `http.Agent`
# and an `https.Agent` whose `createConnection` is wrapped to inject
# `lookup: pinnedLookup`, a resolver that ignores the hostname and answers with
# the address that validates, in both shapes Node's lookup contract allows
# (`options.all` array and the `(address, family)` callback); and each caller
# passes the pair as `httpAgent`/`httpsAgent`. `N8nApiClient` does the same for
# its configured `baseUrl` through a request interceptor, and `maxRedirects: 0`
# is added on the authenticated client so a redirect cannot reintroduce a second
# hostname.
#
# The pool row is correct and needs no correction: repo, CVE and SHA all resolve,
# and the row is a GitHub "Merge commit from fork" whose `git show` prints the
# full diff -- not one of the empty-combined-diff shapes. The vulnerable
# `return { valid: true }` is present at the parent and the pinned agents are
# present at the fixed revision, checked in both directions.
#
# What fires without this, measured `--cache off --max-ram 6GB`, union over three
# runs of each revision, over `src/` (206 TypeScript files): **674 findings and
# 31 flags on both revisions, identical**. Three of those findings are
# VYQL-RF-001 rows landing on the exact call sites this CVE is about --
# `chat-handler.ts:120`, `form-handler.ts:413`, `n8n-api-client.ts:443` -- and
# every one of them survives at the fixed revision, because they are the generic
# "tainted URL reaches `code.UrlFetch`" shape and the change does not remove the
# taint, it pins the socket. Their stated reason is also wrong for this code:
# each says "unless path coveredBy core.AllowlistValidation: not satisfied --
# none found anywhere on flows", but an allowlist is exactly what
# `validateWebhookUrl` is; what is missing is that it cannot bind the
# connection. Findings on the vulnerable lines are not coverage of the
# vulnerability, and a rule that reports the patched revision identically is not
# discrimination -- so the rank is not CAUGHT by VYQL-RF-001.
#
# code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck (VYQL-RF-047, minted here) is the vocabulary
# that is missing. Reuse is checked first against every concept that declares
# `vulnerableTo: [request_forgery.Ssrf]`: code.WebhookUrlFetchMissingSsrfGuard is
# "posts to a webhook URL *without* an observed URL guard", the opposite of this
# code, which has one; code.IncompleteIpDenylist (VYQL-RF-004) is a denylist
# missing ranges, and this one is missing none; code.RequestHostUrlValidationBypass
# is validation that trusts the request's own Host header; code.CloudBaseOpenUrlSsrf
# and code.ChamiloUnsafeGuzzleUrlFetch are unvalidated fetches;
# code.ConfigUrlStoredBeforeSchemeValidation is an ordering defect between storing
# and validating; core.AllowlistValidation is a *control* and emitting it here
# would clear findings rather than raise one. The TOCTOU side is checked too --
# code.FileCheck/code.FileUse, code.TempFileDirectoryCreationRace,
# code.LockFileCreateMissingNoFollow, code.PathBasedSandboxExposeBindRace are the
# four CWE-367 concepts in the ontology and all four are filesystem races over a
# path. Nothing in the ontology says "the value that validates is not the
# value that is used because the name resolves twice", which is a weakness
# class, not this product's semantics: it fires on any Node SSRF filter with the
# same shape and has direct analogues in Python (`socket.getaddrinfo` then
# `requests.get(url)`) and Go (`net.LookupIP` then `http.Get(url)`).
#
# The binding is module scope. The filter and the pinning helper are siblings --
# in n8n-mcp both are static methods of one class, and in the plainer idiom both
# are module-level functions -- so a function-scoped binding cannot see them
# together, which is rank 1372's and rank 1377's gap
# (`endpointGuarded`, `internal/engine/engine.go:1160`). Module scope sidesteps
# it here rather than needing it fixed, because the JavaScript module context
# carries the whole file's structured tokens: the fixed revision's module
# context holds 240 tokens including `call_path:http.Agent`,
# `selector:https.Agent`, `selector:proto.createConnection` and
# `prop:lookup=pinnedLookup`, well under the 512-token cap, so nothing the
# negations need is truncated away.
#
# Rank 1380's counterweight is what makes this shippable rather than ATTENTION:
# the fixed revision leaves documented API calls inside the unit it guards. `lookup` is a
# documented option of `net.createConnection`, `http.Agent` and `https.Agent`,
# and constructing an agent is the documented way to give a connection its own
# resolver -- so the negations name `http.Agent`, `https.Agent`,
# `net.connect`/`net.createConnection`, `tls.connect`, `undici.Agent`, a
# `.createConnection` selector and a `lookup=` property, none of which is
# n8n-mcp's vocabulary. Tested by renaming rather than argued: rewriting the
# patched file with `createPinnedAgents` -> `mkAgents`, `pinnedLookup` -> `zz`
# and `PinnedAgents` -> `AgentPair` still reports **0**, so the discrimination
# is on the Node API and not on the fixed revision's helper names.
#
# Module scope brings rank 1412's comment trap, and it is tested here rather
# than assumed: prepending two comment lines to the vulnerable file that name
# `http.Agent({ lookup })`, `net.createConnection`, `https.Agent`,
# `tls.connect` and `prop lookup=pinned` leaves the flag at **1**. The
# negations use `callPath`, `selector` and `prop`, the module-and-range literal
# clauses use exact `literal in [...]`, and the range-regex clause reads
# `node.context.regex`, which is produced from a regex node and so has no comment
# route at all; no clause uses `containsAny` over `node.context.literal` at
# module scope.
#
# It ships as two bindings, not one, because a `presenceNode` `where` clause
# rejects a parenthesised alternative (`predicate left side must be
# node.<field>`) and the range test has two forms -- a prefix handed to
# `startsWith`, and a range-anchored regex. That is worth stating because the
# rejection is silent at scan time: a `.vyql` that fails to compile makes `scan`
# report **zero findings and exit 0**, so the single-binding draft with the
# parenthesised `or` looked exactly like a binding that matched nothing.
#
# Every clause is checked for being load-bearing by dropping it. Dropping all
# three negations makes the *patched* file report 1, so the discrimination lives
# entirely in them and not in the gate. Dropping the `prop` negation alone makes
# the third case below (a validating resolver handed to `axios.get(url, {
# lookup })`) report, and dropping the `callPath`/`selector` negations alone
# makes the fifth case below (`tls.connect({ host: address })`, the other
# documented way to reach the address that validates) report -- so neither
# negation group subsumes the other: one covers pinning the resolver, the other
# covers skipping the resolver entirely. The positive gate is what rejects the
# fourth and sixth cases: the fourth has the ranges but no resolution, the sixth
# has the resolution but no range.
#
# Whole-`src/` A/B on the two checkouts: **675 findings on the vulnerable
# revision and 674 on the patched**, stable over three `--cache off` runs each,
# the single delta being VYQL-RF-047 HIGH at `src/utils/ssrf-protection.ts:1`.
# No other finding moves and no flag count changes.
#
# Collateral is enumerated, not sampled, because a one-file delta on a small
# repository is not evidence. Across the ~120 repositories cloned for this
# campaign there are 87030 JavaScript and TypeScript files. Two supersets of the
# binding's positive gate are enumerated over all of them: **38** files import
# Node's `dns`/`dns/promises`, of which **22** also carry private, loopback or
# link-local address text; and **90** files contain a `dns`-shaped module string
# at all, of which **23** also make a `lookup`/`resolve`/`resolve4`/`resolve6`
# call. Their union, excluding this rank's own target and probe copies, is
# **21 files**, which is the whole reachable set. Every one of the 21 is scanned
# individually.
#
# **Twenty of the 21 report 0, and the twenty-first is a true positive in another
# repository, found by the binding and read afterwards.** n8n's own
# `packages/@n8n/ai-workflow-builder.ee/evaluations/cli/webhook.ts` has the same
# defect as n8n-mcp and is not the subject of this CVE:
# `validateWebhookUrlWithDns(webhookUrl)` calls `dns.resolve` and `dns.resolve6`,
# runs every returned address through `isPrivateIp`, throws if any is private --
# and then `sendWebhookNotification` calls `fetch(webhookUrl, ...)` on the
# hostname, with no agent and no pinned resolver. That file is never opened
# while authoring the binding; it surfaced because widening the range test to
# regex form brought `/^127\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}$/` into reach. Finding an
# unrelated project's instance of the class is the genericity test passing on
# real code rather than on argument.
#
# Two of the twenty zeroes are the interesting negatives. n8n's
# `@n8n/backend-network/src/ssrf/ssrf-protection.service.ts` is a correct
# implementation of exactly this control -- its doc comment says the custom
# lookup "can be passed to `socket.connect({ lookup })` or
# `axios.request({ lookup })`" -- but it is out of reach because it classifies
# ranges through a library rather than through address text, so it is a true
# negative for the wrong reason and is recorded as such rather than claimed as
# proof of the negation. Vite's `packages/vite/src/node/utils.ts` is a genuine
# false positive in the first revision: `getLocalhostAddressIfDiffersFromDNS`
# calls `dns.lookup('localhost')` and rewrites `'127.0.0.1'` in a dev-server
# URL, which satisfied a literal list that included whole addresses. Narrowing
# the list from whole addresses (`127.0.0.1`, `::1`, `169.254.169.254`) to
# *range prefixes and range regexes* (`127.`, `10.`, `192.168.`, `^10\.`,
# `^169\.254\.`, ...) took vite to 0 while leaving n8n-mcp at 1, because
# rewriting one address is not classifying a range. That is the difference
# between using a special-use address and testing for one. The remaining
# eighteen zeroes are three yarn release bundles, three copies of one Misskey
# OAuth provider, a matrix-appservice-irc test harness, a lobe-chat launcher
# script, and eight n8n SSRF test files -- which matters because
# `inTestOrSeedFile` is inert, so a test fixture has to be excluded by the
# clauses themselves rather than by its path, and here it is: a test asserting
# that a filter blocks `10.0.0.1` neither imports `dns` nor tests a range.
#
# The honest limit, stated rather than hidden: a filter that classifies ranges
# through `ipaddr.js`, `ip-address`, `netmask` or a hand-rolled integer
# comparison carries no address text and is missed -- n8n's own correct service
# is the example, and a vulnerable filter written the same way would be too.
# That is the recall direction, which is the safe one, and it is what keeps the
# binding off every module that merely mentions an address.

test "rank1418: an SSRF host filter resolves the hostname itself, tests the resolved address against private ranges, and returns only a verdict"
  lang javascript
  file src/utils/ssrf-protection.ts
  expect VYQL-RF-047
  expect_review code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck
  code
  ```
  import { URL } from 'url';
  import { lookup } from 'dns/promises';

  const CLOUD_METADATA = new Set([
    '169.254.169.254',
    '169.254.170.2',
    'metadata.google.internal',
  ]);

  const PRIVATE_IP_RANGES = [
    /^10\./,
    /^192\.168\./,
    /^172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[0-1])\./,
    /^169\.254\./,
    /^127\./,
  ];

  export class SSRFProtection {
    static async validateWebhookUrl(urlString: string): Promise<{
      valid: boolean;
      reason?: string
    }> {
      try {
        const url = new URL(urlString);

        if (!['http:', 'https:'].includes(url.protocol)) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Invalid protocol. Only HTTP/HTTPS allowed.' };
        }

        const hostname = url.hostname.toLowerCase();

        if (CLOUD_METADATA.has(hostname)) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Cloud metadata endpoint blocked' };
        }

        let resolvedIP: string;
        try {
          const { address } = await lookup(hostname);
          resolvedIP = address;
        } catch (error) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'DNS resolution failed' };
        }

        if (CLOUD_METADATA.has(resolvedIP)) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Hostname resolves to cloud metadata endpoint' };
        }

        if (resolvedIP === '::1' || resolvedIP.startsWith('127.')) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Localhost access is blocked in strict mode' };
        }

        if (PRIVATE_IP_RANGES.some(regex => regex.test(resolvedIP))) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Private IP addresses not allowed' };
        }

        return { valid: true };
      } catch (error) {
        return { valid: false, reason: 'Invalid URL format' };
      }
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1418: the same filter returning the resolved address and building agents that pin the connection to it"
  lang javascript
  file src/utils/ssrf-protection.ts
  reject_review code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck
  code
  ```
  import { URL } from 'url';
  import { lookup } from 'dns/promises';
  import http from 'http';
  import https from 'https';

  export interface PinnedAgents {
    httpAgent: http.Agent;
    httpsAgent: https.Agent;
  }

  export interface WebhookUrlValidationResult {
    valid: boolean;
    reason?: string;
    address?: string;
    family?: 4 | 6;
  }

  const CLOUD_METADATA = new Set([
    '169.254.169.254',
    '169.254.170.2',
    'metadata.google.internal',
  ]);

  const PRIVATE_IP_RANGES = [
    /^10\./,
    /^192\.168\./,
    /^172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[0-1])\./,
    /^169\.254\./,
    /^127\./,
  ];

  export class SSRFProtection {
    static async validateWebhookUrl(urlString: string): Promise<WebhookUrlValidationResult> {
      try {
        const url = new URL(urlString);

        if (!['http:', 'https:'].includes(url.protocol)) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Invalid protocol. Only HTTP/HTTPS allowed.' };
        }

        const hostname = url.hostname.toLowerCase();

        if (CLOUD_METADATA.has(hostname)) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Cloud metadata endpoint blocked' };
        }

        let resolvedIP: string;
        let resolvedFamily: 4 | 6;
        try {
          const { address, family } = await lookup(hostname);
          resolvedIP = address;
          resolvedFamily = family === 6 ? 6 : 4;
        } catch (error) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'DNS resolution failed' };
        }

        if (CLOUD_METADATA.has(resolvedIP)) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Hostname resolves to cloud metadata endpoint' };
        }

        if (resolvedIP === '::1' || resolvedIP.startsWith('127.')) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Localhost access is blocked in strict mode' };
        }

        if (PRIVATE_IP_RANGES.some(regex => regex.test(resolvedIP))) {
          return { valid: false, reason: 'Private IP addresses not allowed' };
        }

        return { valid: true, address: resolvedIP, family: resolvedFamily };
      } catch (error) {
        return { valid: false, reason: 'Invalid URL format' };
      }
    }

    static createPinnedAgents(address: string, family: 4 | 6): PinnedAgents {
      const pinnedLookup = (_hostname: string, options: any, callback: any): void => {
        if (options && options.all) {
          callback(null, [{ address, family }]);
        } else {
          callback(null, address, family);
        }
      };

      const httpAgent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: false });
      const httpsAgent = new https.Agent({ keepAlive: false });

      const wrap = <A extends http.Agent>(agent: A): A => {
        const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(agent);
        const original = proto.createConnection;
        (agent as any).createConnection = function (options: any, cb: any) {
          return original.call(this, { ...options, lookup: pinnedLookup }, cb);
        };
        return agent;
      };

      return { httpAgent: wrap(httpAgent), httpsAgent: wrap(httpsAgent) };
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1418: a filter that validates inside the resolver the client passes to the request, so the connection uses the address it checked"
  lang javascript
  file src/utils/guarded-lookup.js
  reject_review code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck
  code
  ```
  const dns = require('dns/promises');
  const axios = require('axios');

  const PRIVATE = [/^10\./, /^192\.168\./, /^169\.254\./, /^127\./];

  async function guardedLookup(hostname, options, callback) {
    const { address, family } = await dns.lookup(hostname);
    if (PRIVATE.some((re) => re.test(address))) {
      callback(new Error('blocked address'));
      return;
    }
    callback(null, address, family);
  }

  async function fetchUrl(urlString) {
    return axios.get(urlString, { lookup: guardedLookup, maxRedirects: 0 });
  }

  module.exports = { fetchUrl };
  ```

test "rank1418: an allowlist over an address the caller holds, which resolves nothing and so has no second resolution to disagree with"
  lang javascript
  file src/utils/ip-allowlist.js
  reject_review code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck
  code
  ```
  const PRIVATE = [/^10\./, /^192\.168\./, /^172\.16\./, /^169\.254\./, /^127\./];

  function isPrivateAddress(address) {
    if (address === '::1' || address.startsWith('127.')) return true;
    return PRIVATE.some((re) => re.test(address));
  }

  function assertPeerAllowed(socket) {
    if (isPrivateAddress(socket.remoteAddress)) {
      throw new Error('peer address not allowed');
    }
  }

  module.exports = { isPrivateAddress, assertPeerAllowed };
  ```

test "rank1418: a filter that connects to the address it validated rather than handing the hostname back to the transport"
  lang javascript
  file src/utils/direct-connect.js
  reject_review code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck
  code
  ```
  const dns = require('dns/promises');
  const tls = require('tls');

  const PRIVATE = [/^10\./, /^192\.168\./, /^169\.254\./, /^127\./];

  async function connectToValidated(urlString) {
    const url = new URL(urlString);
    const { address } = await dns.lookup(url.hostname);
    if (PRIVATE.some((re) => re.test(address))) {
      throw new Error('blocked address');
    }
    return tls.connect({ host: address, port: Number(url.port) || 443, servername: url.hostname });
  }

  module.exports = { connectToValidated };
  ```

test "rank1418: a resolver that looks a name up for its records rather than to classify the address it returns"
  lang javascript
  file src/utils/mail-domain.js
  reject_review code.UnpinnedResolvedAddressCheck
  code
  ```
  const dns = require('dns/promises');

  async function mailHostsFor(domain) {
    const records = await dns.resolveMx(domain);
    const hosts = [];
    for (const record of records.sort((a, b) => a.priority - b.priority)) {
      const { address } = await dns.lookup(record.exchange);
      hosts.push({ exchange: record.exchange, address });
    }
    return hosts;
  }

  module.exports = { mailHostsFor };
  ```
